## Part 1 - @Configuration and @Bean

```java
@Configuration  
// Tells Spring → this class contains Bean definitions
// Spring reads this class at startup

public class RedisConfig {

    @Bean
    // Tells Spring → this method returns an object
    // put it in ApplicationContext
    // Other classes can @Autowired it
    public RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory factory) {
```

`RedisConnectionFactory` is **auto injected** by Spring Boot.
It holds → host, port, password of your Redis server from `application.properties`.

```properties
spring.redis.host=localhost
spring.redis.port=6379
# factory reads these automatically
```

---

## Part 2 - RedisCacheConfiguration

This is the **rules object**. It defines HOW cache should behave.

### defaultCacheConfig()

```java
RedisCacheConfiguration defaultConfig = RedisCacheConfiguration
    .defaultCacheConfig()
```

`defaultCacheConfig()` gives you a starting config with:
```
TTL          → no expiry (lives forever)
KeyPrefix    → enabled (cacheName::key)
NullValues   → allowed
Serializer   → JDK serialization (ugly bytes)
```

You then **override** what you want to change.

---

### entryTtl()

```java
.entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(10))
```

**TTL = Time To Live.** After this time Redis **auto deletes** the key.

```
Duration.ofSeconds(30)    → 30 seconds
Duration.ofMinutes(10)    → 10 minutes
Duration.ofHours(1)       → 1 hour
Duration.ofDays(1)        → 1 day
Duration.ZERO             → never expires (careful!)
```

What happens in Redis:
```bash
SET deptStats::HR-2024  "data"  EX 3600
#                                  ↑
#                             Redis TTL in seconds
```

---

### serializeValuesWith()

This is the most important part!

```java
.serializeValuesWith(
    RedisSerializationContext.SerializationPair.fromSerializer(
        new GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer()
    )
)
```

This decides **how your Java object is converted** before storing in Redis.

#### Without this (default JDK serialization):
```
Redis stores:
deptStats::HR-2024 → ¬í sr java.util.ArrayListx...
                      ← UGLY UNREADABLE BYTES
```

#### With JSON serializer:
```
Redis stores:
deptStats::HR-2024 → [{"department":"HR","count":12,"avgSalary":65000}]
                      ← CLEAN READABLE JSON ✅
```

Why JSON is better:
```
✅ Human readable (debug easily)
✅ Works across different Java versions
✅ Other services (Python, Node) can read same cache
❌ Slightly larger size than bytes
```

